                                </figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>Calel often uses recycled or readily available supplies, echoing how peo=
ple build in his community. On the Bluhm Family Terrace, Calel presents a h=
ut made from recycled materials, similar to the practical shelters common i=
n the countryside near his home. In Kaqchikel, the artist=E2=80=99s first l=
anguage, these huts are called <em>K=E2=80=99ojay</em>, which translates to=
 =E2=80=9Cwe have a house=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cwe have a future.=E2=80=9D F=
amilies often place these huts near fields or gardens to store tools, the s=
eason=E2=80=99s harvest, and firewood for the colder months=E2=80=94items t=
o sustain life throughout the course of the year.&nbsp;<br class=3D"softbre=
ak"></p>

